+## Open peer proposal: teach cardinal connectivity before diagonal-touch queries
+
+For generator SHA-256 `1bb0029217dfe3bc4d19aeed64933a5d41db97231c4596a74253544ff650e5b9`
+and canonical rule `2855526e979d`, one native production lesson has a concrete
+connectivity ambiguity. With seed 1, no overrides/difficulty, 3 demonstrations
+and 4 queries, accepted sample indices are 0 through 6. The demonstrations are
+`public_2`, `top_single`, and `count_1_2`. A rival that joins diagonally touching
+red cells (eight-connectivity), retaining the canonical orientation classifier,
+count caps, and output positions, matches every demonstration. It disagrees on
+query index 1 (sample index 4, `public_6`): two bottom-bar T objects touch
+diagonally, so the rival counts two bottom objects overall instead of three.
+Only output cell (0,2) changes, from canonical green 3 to rival background 0.
+
+`checks/peers/e971e0773705/result.json` retains all three original demonstrations,
+the disagreeing query, both executable Racket programs, source/runtime hashes,
+accepted indices, and all seven pair comparisons. Labels and rival predictions
+were replayed through the live protected Racket evaluator; native generation and
+the probe exited 0. Reproduce from the outer packet with:
+
+```sh
+WORK/.venv/bin/python review.py run python3 ../checks/peers/e971e0773705/probe.py
+```
+
+Proposed repair: select a demonstration that distinguishes cardinal from diagonal
+connectivity whenever a query relies on that distinction. This is a lesson-level
+teaching gap despite aggregate profile coverage. Scope is one native lesson and
+one rival; no prevalence or universal-uniqueness claim. Selected generator source
+is unchanged, and this finding/repair recommendation remains an owner-review
+proposal; no approval or shared audit ledger was changed.
